<section class="sec sec-o">
  <div style="max-width:760px;margin-bottom:40px;">
    <p class="eye">The Challenge</p>
    <h2 class="sh">Senior living communities overspend<br><em>on water in three specific ways.</em></h2>
    <p class="sub">The combination of continuous residential occupancy, commercial kitchen and laundry operations, and healthcare-grade water system requirements creates billing complexity and operational waste that few operators have ever systematically addressed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prob-grid"><div class="prob-card"><div class="prob-num">01</div><div class="prob-title">Commercial laundry systems running on manufacturer-default water levels — significantly over-filling</div><p class="prob-body">Commercial laundry equipment in senior living communities typically runs 8&ndash;16 hours per day, seven days a week. Equipment programmed at manufacturer-default water fill levels uses 30&ndash;50% more water per cycle than is required for the load sizes actually being processed. Without a laundry audit to measure actual fill requirements against programmed settings, the excess water consumption accumulates continuously — $8,000&ndash;$20,000 per year in a 150-bed community with on-site laundry.</p></div><div class="prob-card"><div class="prob-num">02</div><div class="prob-title">HVAC and cooling system water not sub-metered — sewer charges on non-returned volumes</div><p class="prob-body">Senior living communities with centralised air conditioning and cooling towers face the same sewer-on-evaporated-water problem as hotels and office buildings — but it's even less likely to have been addressed. Cooling tower make-up water in a 200-unit assisted living community represents a significant annual sewer overcharge that is almost never identified without a specific billing forensics review.</p></div><div class="prob-card"><div class="prob-num">03</div><div class="prob-title">Billing complexity across multi-building campuses never reconciled</div><p class="prob-body">Continuing Care Retirement Communities (CCRCs) often have multiple buildings — independent living, assisted living, memory care, skilled nursing — on the same campus, each with separate utility accounts. Cross-billing between accounts, shared mechanical systems not properly allocated, and meters installed during specific building phases but never reconciled create systematic billing errors that compound over years without being identified.</p></div></div>
</section>
